The back face now: 黑绘夜蓝 body with the tightened back copy ("把事推到终点的力气很稀缺。停下不是被替代，是下一程的起跑。" — 29字), no static frame around the card... but I notice: there's still a visible **beige empty block below the night-blue body** (from ~y 520 to 620). That's the front face peeking? No — when flipped, the front face is rotated -180deg (hidden behind, backface-visibility hidden). The empty beige area below the night blue section... 

Looking at the back face: art (with 1.5px border-bottom) + .body (night blue, min-height?). The beige block below — that's likely the face itself: face bg = var(--gg-card) beige; body has night-blue but the body doesn't fill the face's remaining height. Why is the face taller than its content? The faces are absolutely positioned (`.flip-card .face { position: absolute; inset: 0 }` presumably per default styles), so BOTH faces get the flip-card's height — which is set by... the front face is position absolute too? Then what sets card height? Default styles: `.flip-card { position: relative; ... }` and maybe min-height 660px at narrow width (line 392: `.flip-card { min-height: 660px; }` inside @media max-width 420?). Our viewport 390 → min-height 660px applies! The back face content (art 270 + body ~250) < 660, so the face stretches to 660 and the remaining area shows the face's beige background... but wait, back face body bg night blue — body height is content-height, face bg beige shows below. On the front screenshot earlier (03) there was also beige below the body? Front: body text ends ~y610, card ends ~640 — slight. Actually front card looked fine because content filled more.

Hmm — but ALSO: is that beige block maybe the FRONT face showing through? No: front face is rotateY(-180) with backface-visibility hidden.

So the artifact now: bottom beige filler on the back face because of `min-height: 660px` on .flip-card (default styles.css narrow-screen rule). Fix in skin: `.gg-result .flip-card { min-height: 0; }` — but wait, why does the canonical have min-height 660? To keep both faces equal height / avoid card collapse during flip (both faces absolute → container needs explicit height; min-height provides it in default template where faces are absolute). If faces are absolute, container height comes from...? If faces are absolute, the flip-card has NO in-flow content → height would collapse to min-height 660. That's why! The default relies on min-height 660px.

So I can't just zero it — the card would collapse. The height must match the tallest face's content. Options:
1. Keep min-height but reduce: the tall content was front (modes added) — heights vary per result → fixed 660 either clips (overflow hidden on face — content cut!) or leaves gap. Default uses 660 fixed; content longer than 660? face overflow... default .result-card overflow hidden. With tightened copy, content < 660 → beige gap at bottom (what we see).
2. Better: make faces NOT absolute? Changing positioning breaks the 3D flip (needs absolute stacking).
3. Set min-height dynamically via JS: after render, measure face scrollHeights, set card height = max(front, back). Doable in bind(): card.style.height = Math.max(front.scrollHeight, back.scrollHeight) + 'px'. Faces absolute inset 0 → they stretch to card height; then the face bg fills exactly. But face content shorter than card height → body bg doesn't fill (body is in normal flow inside face, face is taller). For the back face: body night-blue ends, face bg beige below — same issue unless face bg matches body bg per side! Set face.back background: var(--gg-ink) (instead of beige) → the stretch area is night blue = seamless. And face.front bg beige = matches front body. THEN a uniform height is fine — the fill color just extends.

So simplest robust fix: 
- skin.css: `.face.back { background: var(--gg-ink); }` (instead of beige) — the extra stretch area becomes night blue, seamless with body. Front: face bg beige matches body area (body transparent) ✓.
- JS: set card height to max of the two faces' natural heights so the gap isn't huge 660: measure: temporarily? Both faces absolute → their natural content height = scrollHeight (content height). Set card.style.minHeight = Math.max(frontFace.scrollHeight, backFace.scrollHeight) + "px". scrollHeight of absolute element = its content height ✓ (it stretches to card height 660 currently though — scrollHeight would report max(content, client)... face clientHeight = 660 (stretched). Hmm — scrollHeight returns content height if larger, else clientHeight (660). So measuring after removing min-height: set card.style.minHeight='0' then measure scrollHeight (now faces collapse to content height? absolute element with inset:0 → height = card height = 0 → content overflows, scrollHeight = content). Set card height = max(scrollHeights). Then restore. Slight layout thrash but one-time per render.

Simpler alternative: skip JS; keep min-height 660 but make back face bg night-blue (seamless). Front face: beige fill below body — front body bg transparent on beige face → seamless too ✓. The only oddity: card is uniformly 660 tall with extra empty space at bottom (big on back). The earlier canonical design accepted 660. But with tightened copy the emptiness is bigger. The back screenshot: art+body ≈ 520, filler ≈ 140px — noticeable.

Middle ground without JS: reduce min-height via skin (e.g. 560?) — but content varies per result (modes row + long readings ~ front taller; back shorter). With tightened copy front ≈ art 270 + body ~330 = 600; back ≈ 270 + 250 = 520. min-height 620 would clip front? Overflow hidden on face — front content 600 < 620 ok; but other goddesses vary (Athena reading 75字 vs Nike 60; modes row fixed). Risky.

JS approach is cleanest: set card height = max(frontContent, backContent). Let me implement in bind():

```js
/* 卡高 = 两面内容较高者（faces 绝对定位，容器需要显式高度） */
const fitCard = () => {
  card.style.minHeight = "0";
  const h = Math.max(frontFace.scrollHeight, backFace.scrollHeight);
  card.style.height = h + "px";
};
fitCard();
```
But faces absolute with inset 0 → their clientHeight = card height (660 initially, then set). scrollHeight when content < client = clientHeight... circular. Order: set card.style.height = "auto" → card collapses to 0 (faces absolute) → faces' scrollHeight = content height (since face height forced to 0 via inset 0? absolute inset:0 with card auto height → card height 0, face height 0, scrollHeight = content height ✓). Then set card.style.height = max + "px".

Sequence: card.style.height = "0px" (or auto); measure both scrollHeight; set final height. Also on mode switch, front reading length changes → re-run fitCard! Hook into mode switch handler (call fitCard after textContent swap). Also images: art img loads async — art height? `.flip-card .art` — default CSS gives .art fixed height? Line 327-335: `.flip-card .art { ... }` — check: `.flip-card .art img { height: 100%... }` implies .art has explicit height. If .art height is fixed (e.g. 300px), image loading doesn't change layout → measure after render is safe. The default `.flip-card { min-height: 660px }` though — my skin overrides min-height: 0? I set `padding: 0; background: transparent` — must also add `min-height: 0` and let JS set height. And IMPORTANT: reduce-motion fallback / no-JS: if JS fails, height 0 = invisible card! Guard: set a CSS fallback min-height via skin (e.g. 560px) then JS refines: card.style.minHeight = "0"; card.style.height = h. If JS runs, fine. E2E stub: getComputedStyle absent; my bind uses style props + scrollHeight — stub El has no scrollHeight → undefined → Math.max(undefined, undefined) = NaN → card.style.height = "NaNpx" — harmless in stub (no layout). But wait — in stub, does anything assert card visible? Test asserts DOM presence only ✓. OK.

Also `window.getComputedStyle` not needed.

Hmm, but hold on — is this over-engineering? Alternative accepted look: keep canonical min-height 660 but recolor faces per side (seamless fill). The empty area reads as intentional negative space at the card's foot — like the art prints' white margin. In the back screenshot, honestly the beige block below night-blue looks like a design element (card with two-tone back: art + night text panel + beige foot). It's... actually not bad? But user just complained about weird frames — an inconsistent filler block may trigger the same reaction. The JS fit is 10 lines and makes the card hug content like a real print. I'll do the JS fit + per-side face bg.

Implementation:
skin.css: `.face { background: var(--gg-card); }` (front ✓), `.face.back { background: var(--gg-ink); }` add. `.flip-card { min-height: 0; height: auto; }` — wait default 660 comes from styles.css @media narrow — override in skin: `body[data-skin="greek_goddess"] .gg-result .flip-card { min-height: 0; }` (specificity wins). Then bind fitCard.

