```js
const fitCard = () => {
  card.style.height = "0px";
  const target = Math.max(frontFace.scrollHeight, backFace.scrollHeight);
  card.style.height = target + "px";
};
fitCard();
```
frontFace/backFace = card.querySelector(".front"/".back") already exist. And call fitCard() again inside mode-switch handler after textContent change.

Stub safety: frontFace.scrollHeight undefined → Math.max(undefined,undefined)=NaN → height "NaNpx" → stub fine (El.style is {} object). No test breakage. But let me guard: Number.isFinite(target) ? set : leave. 

Edge: reduced-motion/flip mid-animation refit not needed (content static).

Also the face box-shadow with height change — fine.

Then re-screenshot back. Also front after refit (card height = max(front,back) — front content is taller with modes, so front view shows card hugging content ✓; back shows night-blue fill to the SAME height as front (both faces stretch to card height) — back will have night-blue empty area below body (since face.back bg = ink) — consistent, like the print's dark margin. That's the tradeoff of equal-height faces; visually fine (dark foot).

Wait actually — since card height = max(front, back) and front is the taller one typically (modes + longer copy), back gets night-blue filler ≈ 80px. Acceptable, uniform color. Good.

Also add this fitCard behavior note to skin.js comment.

Let me implement: edit skin.css (.face.back bg + flip-card min-height:0) and skin.js (fitCard + call in mode handler). Then reload browser, re-run to result... ugh, 24 more answers (~25s). Fine. Then screenshot front + flip back. Then commit again + mirror.

